sk."] [66 1 "// startSignals starts the receiver once."] [79 1 "// ownSignalHook, when set, handles a signal pyi sends itself (killSignal of its own pid, raise_signal) before the"] [80 1 "// signal module does (queue 220: faulthandler's handlers, and the default action that ends the process): handled true"] [81 1 "// and the call returns."] [84 1 "// signalTakenHook, when set, says a signal's handler is another module's (queue 220: faulthandler.register without"] [85 1 "// chain replaces the Python handler, as its sigaction does): the Python handler is not tripped."] [88 1 "// deliverSignal is the C handler: held when blocked, else tripped; the wakeup fd written."] [110 1 "// tripSignal marks n pending and wakes the main thread (trip_signal)."] [122 1 "// runPendingSignals is _PyErr_CheckSignalsTstate on the main thread: each tripped signal's Python handler"] [123 1 "// called with the signal and the running frame; an exception from one leaves the rest tripped for later."] [154 1 "// setOSHandler makes the process's action for n follow its Python handler and the mask: notified when a"] [155 1 "// callable handles it or it is blocked, else ignored or the default."] [179 3 "// SIG_DFL is the default action, also for a signal ignored at the start that Go's Reset leaves ignored"] [180 3 "// (py-mods-1122)"] [187 1 "// exitSigint is Modules/main.c's exit_sigint after an unhandled KeyboardInterrupt (queue 231): SIGINT's default action"] [188 1 "// restored and the signal sent to the process, so that its parent sees it killed by SIGINT; else exit status 130."] [194 38 "// delivered to this thread on the call's return; another one's at the latest here"] [199 1 "// killSignal is kill(pid, sig). The own process's signal with a Python handler, not blocked, is delivered here"] [200 1 "// and its handler run before kill returns, as the kernel delivers it to the calling thread before kill returns"] [201 1 "// (and SIGPROF, which Go's runtime keeps for itself, reaches the handler)."] [216 3 "// blocked: pending when kill returns, as the kernel makes it (queue 231: sigpending right after saw nothing, the"] [217 3 "// signal still on its way through Go's handler thread)"] [227 1 "// the interval timers (ITIMER_REAL, ITIMER_VIRTUAL, ITIMER_PROF), counted in wall time"] [241 1 "// setItimer arms which's timer (value 0 disarms), returning the old value and interval."] [260 3 "// disarmed: an expiry not yet handled is dropped, as the kernel's single pending bit is taken by the"] [261 3 "// handler that disarms it"] [282 5 "// a Python handler: delivered here, under the timer's lock, so a disarmed timer sends no more"] [283 5 "// (and SIGPROF, which Go's runtime keeps for itself, reaches it)"] [328 4 "// as at CPython's start: SIGINT's default_int_handler, SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ ignored, the rest SIG_DFL"] [334 4 "// a SIGHUP ignored at the process's start (nohup, a parent's SIG_IGN): SIG_IGN, as CPython's signal_get_set_handlers"] [335 4 "// reads it (py-mods-1122)"] [455 5 "// the C handler runs inside raise(): the Python handler before raise_signal returns"] [478 4 "// set_wakeup_fd(fd, /, *, warn_on_full_buffer=True)"] [587 5 "// only the main thread runs handlers: wait for any arrival"] [591 4 "// until a signal is pending (a stale wake-up token is not one)"] [644 7 "// delivered on unblocking: the default action or nothing"] [703 5 "// the own process's signal (a pidfd of another process times out here, harmlessly) handled"] [704 5 "// before the call returns, as the kernel delivers it"]] :go/pos {:name [1 9] :package [1 1]} :go/semis [[51 82] [58 12] [58 22] [71 35] [105 26] [132 12] [132 22] [145 60] [175 22] [204 40] [221 50] [330 14] [330 24] [372 31] [387 14] [387 24] [389 39] [414 40] [436 26] [450 40] [461 63] [497 37] [514 32] [529 31] [571 37] [620 14] [620 24] [649 40] [691 35] [699 104] [706 16] [706 61]]} (go/file "builtins_signal.go" :imports [


